= Optimization — Technical =

'''Optimization''' concerns finding a best feasible decision under an explicitly specified objective, constraints and model. The field includes variational calculus, convex analysis, nonlinear and combinatorial programming, optimal control, robust and stochastic programming, and multiobjective methods. Mathematical optimality is conditional on the stated feasible set and criterion; neither a solver's success flag nor a formal proof is evidence that the model captures every real-world consequence.

This treatment begins with mathematical formulation and convexity, derives Lagrangian duality and Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) conditions, independently solves a constrained quadratic example, distinguishes numerical and discrete algorithms, and addresses uncertainty, interpretation and reproducible verification. Boyd and Vandenberghe's ''Convex Optimization'' (2004) is a foundational scholarly account of convex sets, duality and first-order optimality; Nocedal and Wright's ''Numerical Optimization'' (second edition, 2006) treats iterative methods and general nonlinear programming.<ref name="boyd">Stephen Boyd and Lieven Vandenberghe, [https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/ ''Convex Optimization''], Cambridge University Press (2004); [https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee364a/ Stanford EE364A] course material. Theory of convex problems, duality and KKT conditions.</ref><ref name="nocedal">Jorge Nocedal and Stephen J. Wright, [https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5 ''Numerical Optimization''], second edition, Springer (2006), line search, trust-region, nonlinear constrained and quasi-Newton methods.</ref>

== Optimization as a mathematical decision problem ==

Let <math>x\in\mathbb R^n</math> be continuous decision variables. A standard constrained minimization is

<math display="block">\begin{aligned}\min_{x\in\mathbb R^n}\quad&f_0(x)\\\text{subject to}\quad&f_i(x)\le0,\quad i=1,\ldots,m,\\&h_j(x)=0,\quad j=1,\ldots,p.\end{aligned}</math>

The '''feasible set''' is <math>\mathcal F=\{x:f_i(x)\le0,\ h_j(x)=0\}</math>. A '''global minimizer''' <math>x^*</math> satisfies <math>x^*\in\mathcal F</math> and <math>f_0(x^*)\le f_0(x)</math> for all <math>x\in\mathcal F</math>. A local minimizer only guarantees this inequality within some neighborhood of <math>x^*</math> intersected with the feasible set.

For a real engineering system, the vector <math>x</math> may mix capacities, schedules, voltages, control settings or statistical model parameters. Decision variables can have units; feasibility and objective functions must be dimensionally meaningful. Numerical conditioning depends strongly on variable scaling, but simply rescaling the numbers does not change the underlying physical optimization problem when done consistently.

The existence of a minimizer is not automatic. A feasible problem may be unbounded below, as in <math>\min_{x\in\mathbb R}x</math>; a finite infimum may fail to be attained on an open domain, as in <math>\min_{x>0}x</math>. A solver termination report must distinguish optimal, infeasible, unbounded, numerical failure and iteration-limit conditions.

=== Geometry and convexity ===

A set <math>C\subseteq\mathbb R^n</math> is '''convex''' when

<math display="block">\theta x+(1-\theta)y\in C\quad\text{for all }x,y\in C,\quad0\le\theta\le1.</math>

A function <math>f</math> is convex on <math>C</math> if

<math display="block">f(\theta x+(1-\theta)y)\le\theta f(x)+(1-\theta)f(y).</math>

For differentiable convex <math>f</math>, its first-order supporting inequality is

<math display="block">f(y)\ge f(x)+\nabla f(x)^\mathsf T(y-x).</math>

If the feasible set is convex and <math>f_0</math> convex, any local minimizer is global. If the objective is strictly convex on the convex feasible set, any minimizer, if it exists, is unique. For twice-differentiable <math>f</math> on an open convex set, positive semidefinite Hessians are sufficient for convexity; positive definiteness everywhere is a sufficient condition for strict convexity, not a necessary characterization of every strictly convex function.<ref name="boyd"/>

'''Convexity of the objective alone is insufficient''' when the feasible set is nonconvex. Nonlinear equalities, integer requirements, disconnected feasible regions and nonlinear dynamics can fundamentally change what an optimizer can certify.

== Optimality conditions and Lagrangian duality ==

For smooth inequality constraints and equality constraints, define Lagrange multipliers <math>\lambda_i\ge0</math> and unrestricted multipliers <math>\nu_j</math>. The Lagrangian is

<math display="block">\mathcal L(x,\lambda,\nu)=f_0(x)+\sum_{i=1}^m\lambda_i f_i(x)+\sum_{j=1}^p\nu_j h_j(x).</math>

The dual function is the infimum of the Lagrangian over the specified primal domain:

<math display="block">g(\lambda,\nu)=\inf_{x\in\mathcal D}\mathcal L(x,\lambda,\nu),\qquad \lambda\ge0.</math>

For a minimization problem, any dual-feasible <math>(\lambda,\nu)</math> produces a lower bound <math>g(\lambda,\nu)\le p^*</math> on the best primal objective <math>p^*</math>, a result called '''weak duality'''. The dual optimum is <math>d^*=\sup_{\lambda\ge0,\nu}g(\lambda,\nu)</math>, and <math>d^*\le p^*</math>. Strong duality is the more specific equality <math>d^*=p^*</math>, which requires suitable assumptions.

In a convex problem whose inequality functions are convex, equality functions affine, and with appropriate regularity such as Slater's strict-feasibility condition, strong duality holds under the standard theorem assumptions. Slater's condition is sufficient in that setting, but not a universal necessary requirement for every convex problem. Constraint qualifications determine when Lagrange multipliers and KKT conditions are valid descriptions of optimum points.<ref name="boyd"/>

The KKT conditions for differentiable data are:

<math display="block">\begin{aligned}
\nabla_x\mathcal L(x^*,\lambda^*,\nu^*)&=0 &&\text{(stationarity)},\\
f_i(x^*)&\le0,\ \ h_j(x^*)=0 &&\text{(primal feasibility)},\\
\lambda_i^*&\ge0 &&\text{(dual feasibility)},\\
\lambda_i^*f_i(x^*)&=0 &&\text{(complementary slackness)}.
\end{aligned}</math>

Under the convex assumptions, these conditions are sufficient for a global solution; with strong duality and appropriate regularity they characterize an optimum. For nonconvex problems, KKT conditions are at best necessary under constraint qualifications and are not sufficient for global optimality without additional structure. A stationarity residual near zero alone does not establish feasibility, absence of a better basin, or numerical accuracy.

== Independently worked benchmark: constrained quadratic projection ==

Consider a two-variable convex problem with dimensionless pedagogical variables:

<math display="block">\begin{aligned}
\min_{x,y}\quad &(x-3)^2+(y-1)^2\\
\text{subject to}\quad&x+y\le3,\qquad x\ge0,\quad y\ge0.
\end{aligned}</math>

The unconstrained optimum <math>(3,1)</math> is infeasible because its sum is 4. The objective's Hessian is <math>2I</math>, positive definite; its strictly convex objective and convex closed feasible set imply a unique minimizer exists in this instance. A strictly feasible point such as <math>(1,1)</math> satisfies <math>x+y<3</math> and <math>x,y>0</math>, enabling the usual strong-duality KKT reasoning.

Introduce multiplier <math>\lambda\ge0</math> for <math>x+y-3\le0</math> and <math>\mu_x,\mu_y\ge0</math> for <math>-x\le0,-y\le0</math>. The complete Lagrangian is

<math display="block">\mathcal L=(x-3)^2+(y-1)^2+\lambda(x+y-3)-\mu_xx-\mu_yy.</math>

At a candidate with <math>x,y>0</math>, complementary slackness makes <math>\mu_x=\mu_y=0</math>. The first-order equations give

<math display="block">2(x-3)+\lambda=0,\qquad2(y-1)+\lambda=0,</math>
<math display="block">x=3-\frac{\lambda}{2},\quad y=1-\frac{\lambda}{2},\quad x+y=4-\lambda.</math>

The active upper-sum constraint requires <math>4-\lambda=3</math>, so <math>\lambda^*=1</math>. Therefore

<math display="block">(x^*,y^*)=(2.5,0.5),\quad f^*=(-0.5)^2+(-0.5)^2=0.5.</math>

Feasibility is immediate: <math>x^*+y^*=3</math>, <math>x^*,y^*>0</math>, and all multipliers obey their signs. The Lagrangian gradient vanishes and complementary slackness holds, establishing global optimality by convex KKT. Since the objective is strictly convex, this minimizer is unique.

=== Dual certificate and sensitivity meaning ===

Ignoring the nonnegativity inequalities temporarily while retaining the upper-sum inequality gives the relaxed Lagrangian

<math display="block">\mathcal L_0(x,y,\lambda)=(x-3)^2+(y-1)^2+\lambda(x+y-3).</math>

The infimum over unrestricted <math>(x,y)</math> occurs at <math>x=3-\lambda/2</math>, <math>y=1-\lambda/2</math>, so its dual function is

<math display="block">g_0(\lambda)=\lambda-\frac{\lambda^2}{2},\qquad\lambda\ge0.</math>

The unique maximum over <math>\lambda\ge0</math> is at <math>\lambda^*=1</math>, with <math>g_0(1)=0.5</math>. Since the associated minimizer is nonnegative, this relaxed dual certificate achieves the primal value for the full constrained problem. The lower bound and feasible upper bound match at 0.5: this is a transparent independent optimality proof.

The multiplier's local interpretation is a '''shadow price''' of the upper-sum constraint under suitable regularity. If the bound becomes <math>x+y\le b</math> near <math>b=3</math> and the active set remains unchanged, the optimum-value derivative is <math>dp^*(b)/db=-\lambda^*=-1</math>. This is a sensitivity statement about a mathematical bound, not a dollar price unless the objective and constraint have suitable units. It may change at active-set transitions.<ref name="boyd"/>

