A computational solver should reproduce the optimal objective and a feasible decision to its declared numerical tolerance; a solver report alone is weaker evidence than matching this independent analytic certificate.

== Numerical algorithms and convergence claims ==

A basic unconstrained gradient-descent iteration is

<math display="block">x_{k+1}=x_k-\alpha_k\nabla f(x_k).</math>

For <math>f(x)=(x-3)^2</math> with constant <math>\alpha</math>, the error evolves as

<math display="block">e_{k+1}=(1-2\alpha)e_k,\qquad e_k=x_k-3.</math>

Thus <math>0<\alpha<1</math> makes this ideal deterministic one-dimensional iteration contract. At <math>\alpha=0.25</math> and <math>x_0=0</math>, <math>x_1=1.5</math>, <math>x_2=2.25</math>. But if the genuine constraint is <math>0\le x\le2</math>, unprojected gradient descent eventually becomes infeasible; convergence to the unconstrained optimum 3 solves the '''wrong''' problem.

A projected gradient step for a closed convex feasible set <math>C</math> is <math>x_{k+1}=\Pi_C[x_k-\alpha_k\nabla f(x_k)]</math>. For <math>C=[0,2]</math>, <math>\alpha=0.25</math>, the iterates from zero are 1.5 and then 2, yielding the constrained global optimum <math>x^*=2</math> with objective 1. This demonstration distinguishes iteration correctness from optimization-model correctness.

In higher-dimensional smooth problems, line-search and trust-region methods control steps and address numerical failure; Newton and quasi-Newton methods use curvature information and can converge more rapidly locally under favorable regularity. Nocedal and Wright analyze these algorithms along with sequential quadratic programming and constrained methods.<ref name="nocedal"/>

An algorithm described as "convergent" needs a specific meaning: objective values, iterates, stationarity residual, feasible gap or global optimality under a given model and assumptions. Ill-conditioning, nonsmoothness, discontinuities, noise and saddle points can invalidate casual extrapolations from a textbook quadratic example.

== Linear, integer and combinatorial optimization ==

Linear programming minimizes a linear objective under affine constraints. Its feasible geometry is a polyhedron; optimal solutions, when they exist, may lie on a face with multiple minimizers. Simplex-type algorithms traverse a structured set of candidate bases, whereas interior-point methods approach solutions through different numerical strategies. Formal worst-case complexity, expected runtime and observed solver behavior are distinct.

Integer and mixed-integer programming introduce discrete feasibility. A relaxation that allows integer variables to take continuous values can provide a lower bound for a minimization, while the best currently known feasible integer solution gives an upper bound. Branch-and-bound and cutting-plane strategies can tighten these bounds until an accepted gap criterion is met.

For minimization with feasible incumbent value <math>U</math> and valid lower bound <math>L</math>, the absolute optimality gap is <math>U-L</math> whenever both are valid under the same model. A stated relative gap must define the normalization because different solvers use different conventions, especially near zero or with negative objectives. A small gap certifies model-relative solution quality, not the validity of an omitted constraint. Solver tolerances for feasibility and integrality also matter.

The computational difficulty of an optimization problem depends on its structure and input encoding. One must not claim every nonlinear problem is intractable or every convex problem can be solved in a constant amount of work. [[Computational complexity]] addresses these formal distinctions, and [[Scaling]] studies resource use as problem or workload size changes.

== Stochastic and robust formulations ==

A decision may be made before uncertain conditions <math>\xi</math> are observed. In stochastic optimization with an explicit probability model <math>P</math>, one form is

<math display="block">\min_{x\in C}\ \mathbb E_{\xi\sim P}[f(x,\xi)],</math>

subject to any additional constraints and measurability/integrability conditions. The solution's meaning depends on the selected loss, the true applicability of <math>P</math>, and whether the decision is allowed to adapt after new information arrives.

In robust optimization, a deterministic uncertainty set <math>\mathcal U</math> may instead define a worst-case criterion,

<math display="block">\min_{x\in C}\ \sup_{\xi\in\mathcal U}f(x,\xi).</math>

The two objectives answer different questions. An expected optimum may expose rare severe loss; an overly broad robust set may be excessively conservative or infeasible. Distributionally robust variants optimize against a collection of plausible probability distributions, but these depend on how the ambiguity set is constructed. No mathematical notation substitutes for evidentiary justification of the uncertainty model.

When objectives or constraints derive from measurements, uncertainty propagation and input dependence matter. A solution stable under one parameter sweep can be fragile under structural model changes, and uncertainty about a causal effect is not equivalent to random variability in a simulator.

== Multiobjective optimality and Pareto frontiers ==

For objective vector <math>\mathbf f(x)=(f_1(x),\ldots,f_k(x))</math> to be minimized, a feasible point <math>x^*</math> is '''Pareto efficient''' if no other feasible point improves at least one objective without worsening any other (equivalently, no feasible point weakly improves all objectives and strictly improves at least one). This is a mathematical dominance relation, not the unrelated popular "80/20 rule."

Weighted sums such as

<math display="block">\min_{x\in C}\sum_{i=1}^k w_i f_i(x),\qquad w_i>0,</math>

can identify supported efficient tradeoffs under appropriate assumptions. For a nonconvex objective image, varying weights can miss nonsupported efficient points. Choosing weights embeds value judgments and unit/normalization choices; a numerical optimum cannot establish which societal or safety tradeoff ought to be accepted.

Different stakeholders may prioritize risk, efficiency, equity, energy cost, interpretability or reliability. In consequential applications the admissibility of tradeoffs and mandatory constraints should be defined before solving rather than retrofitted to the optimizer's output.

== Goodhart-type failures and objective validity ==

When a proxy metric becomes a target, decision-makers or algorithms can exploit weaknesses of the proxy. This is not a failure of convex duality or gradient descent; it is a discrepancy between the formal objective and the human, scientific or institutional goal. An "optimal" recommendation engine that maximizes short-term clicks may violate accuracy or safety expectations absent from its constraints.

For an artificial agent, optimization language should correspond to an implemented objective and an observable decision process. A system generating fluent action plans does not prove that it internally solves a specified global optimization problem. Likewise, a human or organization making adaptive choices need not be represented as solving one maximization unless the model and empirical fit justify the claim.

This connects to [[Decision theory]], [[Satisficing]], [[Robustness]], [[Validation]], [[Scientific model]] and [[Uncertainty]]. Useful metrics are always conditional on measurement and design choices.

== Verification, reproducibility and epistemic audit ==

A scholarly optimization result should provide:

* '''Problem identity:''' objective, exact constraint definitions, variable dimensions/units and all implicit domain restrictions.
* '''Data provenance:''' sampling, calibration, preprocessing, parameter versions and any hidden exogenous dependencies.
* '''Model class:''' smooth/convex, linear, mixed-integer, stochastic, robust, dynamic or multiobjective; cite the applicable theory.
* '''Algorithm and environment:''' solver, exact version, initialization, scaling, tolerance, random seeds, runtime and computational resource.
* '''Feasibility:''' largest absolute/relative constraint violation, integrality compliance and sign/unit conventions.
* '''Optimality evidence:''' KKT/dual residuals, bound gap, a verified global certificate or clearly qualified local stopping criterion.
* '''Sensitivity and validity:''' performance under input changes, alternative constraint regimes, independent test scenarios and stakeholder costs.
* '''Adversarial checks:''' whether the apparent objective improvement exploits measurement artifacts or omitted constraints.

Reproduce analytic benchmarks before a large simulation. For the quadratic case, independently verify <math>x+y=3</math>, <math>(x,y)=(2.5,0.5)</math>, objective 0.5, multiplier 1, dual value 0.5 and zero primal–dual gap. An implementation yielding a lower numerical objective while violating a constraint has '''not''' discovered a superior feasible solution.

A program solving its encoded objective accurately is an implementation achievement; asserting that the encoded objective is the right scientific question requires separate experimental, ethical and domain evidence. This distinction is a central limit on interpreting all optimization results.

== Research directions and boundaries ==

Advanced research includes scalable nonlinear and mixed-integer methods, high-dimensional nonconvex landscapes, learning-to-optimize and implicit differentiation, uncertainty-aware decisions, adversarial distribution shift, safe adaptive control, multiobjective preference elicitation and trustworthy optimality certificates for large engineered systems. Each has its own mathematical assumptions and validation standards.

No article can establish a global solution algorithm that is universally tractable for arbitrary nonconvex combinatorial problems. The task is instead to state the solvable structure, choose defensible approximations or relaxations, and expose how much is formally proved versus numerically observed.

== Additional formulation taxonomy and interpretation safeguards ==

A mathematical formulation should report decision variables and units, objective, admissible constraints, and whether a feasible optimum exists. Linear programming assumes linear objectives and constraints; convex optimization can certify global minima under its stated assumptions; nonconvex optimization may return only local solutions; integer/combinatorial models require discrete search and explicit incumbent/bound gaps; stochastic and robust formulations depend on a specified probability model or uncertainty set; multiobjective problems generally require explicit preference or Pareto-front analysis. These categories overlap and are not interchangeable solver labels.

A computational certificate should distinguish primal feasibility, objective value, dual feasibility, duality gap, numerical stopping tolerances and model validity. The existence of a mathematical optimum does not imply ethical adequacy or safety when omitted constraints or affected parties change the decision problem. [[Satisficing]] is a distinct decision criterion, not a claim of global optimality.

